WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.200
Let us revamp your film radar with a comprehensive
list of all the underappreciated movies you

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.540
may have missed in 2019, and are bound to
rave about to everyone you know as soon as

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.400
you finally see them.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.500
Acclaimed director Steven Soderbergh is three
for three of late in the "making movies that

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.620
go underappreciated by most people" game.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.860
He delivered the grossly underrated heist
comedy Logan Lucky in 2017, then dished up

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.020
his psychological horror flick Unsane in 2018.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.019
And now, in 2019, Soderbergh has kept up the
streak with High Flying Bird, a subversion

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.100
of the sports movie genre that's ceaselessly
stylish and respectably risky.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.690
High Flying Bird details the plan sports agent
Ray Burke carries out after he stumbles upon

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.460
a loophole during a professional basketball
league lockdown.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.080
Shot entirely on an iPhone, High Flying Bird
has the kind of sleek aesthetic and structure

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.880
that will grab you by the wrist and send you
down the rabbit hole.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.960
The lovechild of Moneyball and Jerry Maguire,
Bird hit Netflix's enormous content catalogue

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.050
in early February, around the same time as
some of the streamer's more highly publicized

00:00:59.050 --> 00:00:59.660
projects.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.200
Having too many options to choose from is
likely why viewers missed out on the film.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.220
The good news is that the fix to this is as
simple as booting it up on Netflix.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:14.000
One of the greatest superhero films of 2019
was one the vast majority of moviegoers completely

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:14.700
missed out on.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.060
Fast Color, directed and co-written by Miss
Stevens filmmaker Julia Hart, stars The Cloverfield

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:25.200
Paradox actress Gugu Mbatha-Raw as Ruth, whose
seizures cause massive earthquakes, and Orange

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.249
Is the New Black alum Lorraine Toussaint as
Bo, Ruth's mother who has the ability to disintegrate

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:34.820
and reassemble objects with her mind and to
see "the colors" — vivid flashes of light.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:41.380
"After you put together whatever it is you
took apart, everyone sees this object, but

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.660
you see the colors."

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.900
Critics agree that Fast Color is a movie in
a league of its own.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.220
The Movie Critic's Sean P. Means said of the
underappreciated pic:

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.920
"In a movie world overrun with superheroes,
director Julia Hart's independent gem Fast

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:59.100
Color delivers something even more fascinating:
Characters for whom special powers are both

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.900
a curse and a valuable tool."

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.060
In a cinema landscape littered with space
movies, High Life takes a giant leap ahead

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:06.720
of the others in its genre.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.140
Robert Pattinson leads the film as Monte,
a man who is on death row for killing his

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.500
friend when he was a child.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.660
Monte, his fellow inmates, and their criminal
supervisor, Dr. Dibs, are carrying out a dangerous

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:22.080
mission in space, hurtling toward a black
hole for reasons not initially explicitly

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:22.580
known.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.600
The journey doesn't end well, and the path
that the prisoners walk along is just as devastating.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.940
High Life isn't a film for everyone there's
a big divide between the critical rating and

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.080
audience score on Rotten Tomatoes but it's
perfect for those wanting to experience something

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.270
completely new.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.940
As The New Republic's Josephine Livingstone
wrote:

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.880
"High Life will send you back out into the
world with a totally new idea of what black

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:44.660
holes symbolize.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.160
It is an elliptical film, yes, because it
will answer only the questions that you did

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.140
not think to ask."

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.860
Director Zhang Yimou redeemed himself for
the truly terrible 2017 film The Great Wall

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.660
with Shadow, the Chinese epic that critics
say you have to see to believe.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.260
There's so much more to Shadow than meets
the eye, like court intrigue, hopeless romance,

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:08.230
fight sequences that captivate, and weapons
that would make comic book villains drool

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:12.700
with envy, but it's the exploration of yin
and yang that makes the film unforgettable.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.540
MovieFreak.com said of it:

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.090
"Shadow is a masterful spectacle of human
frailty and intellectual dishonesty that only

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.020
grows in resonance as it goes along."

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.400
Exalted by Rolling Stone as, quote, "one of
the best movies of 2019 by a long shot," The

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.120
Last Black Man in San Francisco may just be
the top film of the year.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.500
It's a rousing, soul-stirring, haunting effort
from Joe Talbot, who makes his directorial

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.950
debut with a film that explores identity,
truth, and belonging in a city where a pair

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.620
of best friends no longer feel there's any
space for them.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:48.120
The film which also stars powerhouse talents
Danny Glover, Mike Epps, Tichina Arnold, and

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.940
Rob Morgan left critics moved when A24 released
it in a limited launch on June 7th.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.360
Beautifully constructed, The Last Black Man
in San Francisco bursts with emotion, grapples

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.420
with life truths that will resonate with audiences
of all sorts, and has the makings of an unforgettable

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.260
classic.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.220
Step aside, Jaws.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.760
The best new disaster-film-slash-creature-feature
on the block is Crawl.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.980
Set in Florida during a Category 5 hurricane,
Crawl follows aspiring college swimmer Haley

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.600
as she and her father fend off a horde of
alligators who are out for blood.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.040
As time runs out to flee from Florida, the
threat of drowning and/or being eaten alive

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.780
by the hungry gators grows with each passing
minute.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.280
"Pete, do you hear that?"

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.650
Crawl actually scored a wide release through
Paramount Pictures, but that didn't help the

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.020
film rope in a huge audience.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.160
It went mostly unnoticed at the box office,
raking in only $55 million worldwide.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.280
It did, however, kick up a ton of talk among
critics, who have praised it as, quote, "a

00:04:43.289 --> 00:04:44.800
lean-and-mean thriller."

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.430
Willamette Week said of the film:

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.880
"Who knew this ridiculous premise could be
so beautifully crafted, immaculately paced

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.220
and genuinely shocking?"

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:57.919
A gripping mystery-drama from director Julius
Onah, Luce picks up ten years after the adoption

00:04:57.919 --> 00:05:03.240
of Luce Edgar, a bright boy born in war-torn
Eritrea who has become a respected debater

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:07.280
at his high school, an all-star athlete, and
a beloved member of his Arlington, Virginia

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:07.780
community.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.520
Luce's dazzling reputation takes an unexpected
hit after he submits an essay that causes

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:16.980
immediate concern, one that points to something
far worse than words on a page.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.820
The Detroit News said of the film:

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.260
"This is a powerful film that shows how far
some will go to protect the reality they choose

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:23.760
to believe.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.940
It gets into your head and stays there."

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:31.180
Luce debuted in theaters in early August 2019,
remaining under the radar for the entirety

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:31.700
of its run.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.980
With powerful performances from its cast and
a story that will undoubtedly provoke discussion,

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.280
this film is one to dissect and analyze.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.900
Elements of Madness writer Douglas Davidson
said:

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:47.439
"In a tight 109 minutes, Onah puts into motion
a film which examines ideas of racial coding,

00:05:47.439 --> 00:05:52.069
classism, parenting, academic excellence,
nature vs. nurture, and more, all without

00:05:52.069 --> 00:05:54.420
losing an ounce of power or sincerity."

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.319
Bill Skarsgård and Maika Monroe play against
type a bit in the Dan Berk and Robert Olsen-directed

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.600
comedy-thriller Villains.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.780
Skarsgård — who played Pennywise in the
It movies — and burgeoning scream queen

00:06:05.789 --> 00:06:10.400
Monroe portray Mickey and Jules, a pair of
amateur criminals whose dreamily-discussed

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.719
gas station robbery goes less like a scene
from Pulp Fiction and more like the sinking

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:15.800
of the Titanic.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.660
The couple end up on the run, but when their
car breaks down and they seek refuge at the

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:24.060
home of a seemingly normal couple, a horrifying
discovery in the basement turns their home

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.800
invasion into a whole other crime entirely.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:28.000
"Come on baby, there's nothing down here."

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:33.219
If this sounds like your cup of tea, and you're
now kicking yourself for not having heard

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.860
of Villains while it was out in theaters,
we don't blame you.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.870
Villains, a film that Cinemacy calls a, quote,
"stylish and savage battle of the bad guys,"

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.940
is undoubtedly one of the most fun movies
of 2019.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.539
It's been a hot minute since Eddie Murphy
put out a film worth watching.

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:51.610
Critics put his two most recent films through
the shredder, and it seemed that the actor

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:55.680
would never have a hit again — destined
to be remembered as a talented comedian who

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.120
lost his way, or that dude who voiced Donkey
in the Shrek franchise.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:03.380
"This is gonna be fun, we can stay up late,
swapping manly stories, and in the morning,

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:04.660
I'm making waffles."

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.840
That changed with the launch of Dolemite Is
My Name.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:13.080
This biographical comedy stars Murphy as real-life
actor and singer Rudy Ray Moore, who gained

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.469
fame for his over-the-top character Dolemite
in the blaxploitation films Dolemite, The

00:07:17.469 --> 00:07:19.680
Human Tornado, and The Dolemite Explosion.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.499
The flick follows Murphy's Moore as he discovers
the key to success after a string of failures.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:29.760
Unlike the film within the film, Dolemite
Is My Name is a bona fide smash with critics.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:35.099
But also unlike 1975's Dolemite, the 2019
movie that tells of its creation wasn't a

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.940
box office hit in its limited theatrical release.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.440
It sadly went unseen by the masses, but those
who did catch it had amazing things to say.

00:07:43.449 --> 00:07:45.610
The Washington Post's Ann Hornaday wrote:

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:50.369
"[Eddie Murphy] is back, baby, in a performance
so big and so generous that it virtually busts

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:51.100
through the screen."

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.400
Hopefully, the Netflix-produced biopic will
find more fans on the streaming service.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:00.220
Contrary to what its name suggests, Billy
Senese's mystery-suspense film The Dead Center

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.640
didn't end up in the dead center of moviegoers'
radars.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.780
Where it did end up, however, was on the coveted
list of perfectly rated movies on Rotten Tomatoes

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:09.800
and for good reason.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:15.559
The film stars Primer and Upstream Color director
Shane Carruth as Daniel Forrester, a psychiatrist

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.340
who takes on a catatonic patient who's unable
to recall how he landed in the hospital at

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:19.840
all.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:24.200
As Dr. Forrester attempts to connect with
him, people all across the ward start dropping

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.800
dead and Dr. Forrester soon realizes that
he may have unwittingly unleashed something

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.980
far more chilling than even the worst of his
personal demons.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.240
Luke Y. Thompson at Nerdist gave The Dead
Center a near-perfect score, writing in his

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:36.760
review:

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.360
"Writer-director Bill Senese proves himself
a masterful new voice in terror, specifically

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.830
the body horror kind previously dominated
by David Cronenberg [...] You may come out

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.730
of The Dead Center rooting for a sequel.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:52.050
I'm just rooting for whatever Senese's next
movie is, even (especially?) if it keeps me

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.140
awake at night."

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:58.060
Praised nearly universally by critics for
Elisabeth Moss' lead performance, Her Smell

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:03.280
follows has-been rock superstar Becky Something,
once part of the '90s female grunge band Something

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.940
She, who now finds herself spiraling in the
wake of her former success.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:11.820
Written and directed by Alex Ross Perry, Her
Smell is an unflinchingly de-glamorized look

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.970
at rock and roll stardom and everything that
comes with it, as Becky's fame and success

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:19.200
is consistently undermined by her own acts
of self-sabotage.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:20.220
"You're a mess."

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.100
No, you're a mess..."

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.759
The film challenges audiences who are used
to seeing gritty rock biopics of both real-life

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.000
and fictional stars play out a certain way.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:34.190
The typical tropes of addiction and recovery,
frenzied highs and dismal lows, and the often-tragic

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:38.610
tension between public persona and personal
vulnerability are all there, but not in the

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.700
ways we necessarily expect to see them.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.680
Despite its positive reception from critics,
you wouldn't be alone if you missed Her Smell

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:46.440
during its limited theatrical run.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:50.440
The, quote, "thrilling, funny, and heartbreaking"
film failed to sell enough tickets to even

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:55.110
make up its modest budget, and much like Becky
herself, is in danger of winding up obscure

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:57.440
and forgotten, despite its artistic greatness.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.240
In an increasingly cynical cinematic landscape,
it feels almost subversive that a movie as

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.800
quiet, gentle, and kindhearted as The Peanut
Butter Falcon exists at all.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:11.620
Starring Shia LaBeouf and newcomer Zack Gottsagen,
The Peanut Butter Falcon tells the story of

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:15.649
an unlikely friendship forged between a young
man with Down syndrome who dreams of becoming

00:10:15.649 --> 00:10:19.820
a professional wrestler and the down-on-his-luck
fisherman who agrees to accompany him on his

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:20.320
journey.

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:24.820
Written and directed by Tyler Nilson and Michael
Schwartz, The Peanut Butter Falcon was conceived

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:28.769
specifically for Gottsagen, who wanted to
be an actor but knew he'd have a hard time

00:10:28.769 --> 00:10:29.960
getting cast in Hollywood.

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:34.660
Drawing from many of his own experiences and
ambitions, the Huckleberry Finn-inspired narrative

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:38.560
places him front and center, and Gottsagen
shines as a leading man.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.360
It didn't attract large crowds during its
theatrical release, but for those who did

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:46.339
manage to catch it on the big screen, both
audiences and critics agree that The Peanut

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.400
Butter Falcon was worth the trip to the theater.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.580
Speaking to /Film about Gottsagen, Schwartz
said:

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.280
"[He's] a born entertainer [...] we just sat
down and all wrote out a script, built a dance

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.200
floor, and Zack just went out and danced."

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:04.200
Described by writer and director Taika Waititi
as a, quote, "anti-hate satire," Jojo Rabbit

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:08.170
takes a bold swing for the fences of good
taste in its depiction of a young Nazi boy

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:12.940
named Jojo living through the waning days
of World War II with his imaginary friend,

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:13.600
Adolf Hitler.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:19.050
Waititi, who is himself Jewish, plays the
imaginary Hitler with a hefty dose of irreverence,

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:22.930
mocking the infamous dictator and imbuing
him with a sense of half-witted goofiness

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:27.459
while never shying away from the catastrophic
effects of his horrifying beliefs and policies.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.200
"People used to say a lot of nasty things
about me.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:31.600
'Oh, this guy's a lunatic.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.540
Oh, look at that psycho, he's gonna get us
all killed."

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.880
This is the tricky tonal tightrope Jojo Rabbit
walks throughout its runtime, attempting to

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:43.700
perfectly balance its silly comedy with the
gravity of its subject matter.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:44.980
It shouldn't work.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.259
Reservations about the film's tone divided
critics, and perhaps contributed to keeping

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:53.280
the film's box office take surprisingly modest,
despite its A-list cast.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.480
But audiences seem to agree that filtering
its discordant narrative through the innocent

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:02.639
if deeply misguided eyes of a child helps
Jojo Rabbit find an enjoyable rhythm that,

00:12:02.639 --> 00:12:06.899
despite its wackiness, communicates a timely
and earnest message about the dangers of hate

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.400
and the power of compassion.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:14.120
Based on the 1999 crime novel by Jonathan
Lethem, Motherless Brooklyn follows Lionel

00:12:14.129 --> 00:12:18.170
Essrog, a private investigator with Tourette
syndrome, as he attempts to solve the killing

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.780
of his mentor, Frank, in 1950s New York.

00:12:21.209 --> 00:12:25.060
But digging for the truth winds up unearthing
some secrets that powerful people would rather

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:30.060
keep buried, placing Lionel in the crosshairs
of some of New York's most dangerous players.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:34.240
Motherless Brooklyn has long been a passion
project for Edward Norton, who also wrote,

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:35.819
directed, and produced the film.

00:12:35.819 --> 00:12:41.080
It boasts an impressive cast that also includes
Willem Dafoe, Alec Baldwin, Bobby Cannavale,

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.700
Cherry Jones, and Michael Kenneth Williams.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:47.529
Although the critical reception was somewhat
mixed, which could have contributed to the

00:12:47.529 --> 00:12:51.980
film's disappointing box office performance,
reviews praised the film's strong performances,

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:53.880
atmospheric setting, and smart writing.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.620
Writes critic Alexandra Heller-Nicholas:

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:01.839
"Motherless Brooklyn is a love-letter to the
long-held focus across film noir on masculinity

00:13:01.839 --> 00:13:05.941
in crisis, but through Lethem's source material
and Norton's deep engagement with it, something

00:13:05.941 --> 00:13:09.520
genuinely fresh explodes out of seemingly
familiar material."

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.980
On paper, Honey Boy seems like the type of
film that shouldn't work at all, the type

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:18.310
of self-indulgent and overly personal fare
that is much more therapeutic for the filmmaker

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:20.440
than it is entertaining for an audience.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:25.560
And yet, somehow, the Shia LaBeouf-penned
film, in which he also stars as his own father,

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:30.230
manages to defy expectations, delivering a
deeply personal and introspective movie that

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:35.940
also works as a nuanced examination of a dysfunctional
father-son relationship and a thoughtful commentary

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:37.730
on child actors in Hollywood.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:39.089
"Dad, dad, dad.

00:13:39.089 --> 00:13:40.340
I was getting the scene."

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:42.180
"Yeah, I don't care.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:43.460
Come here.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:44.320
Come here.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.000
Child labor laws!"

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:51.220
In a loose recounting of LaBeouf's own childhood,
Honey Boy mostly follows 12-year-old television

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:56.029
star Otis, who lives his life between comedy
sets and a seedy motel room, which he shares

00:13:56.029 --> 00:14:01.160
with his father, James, a former rodeo clown
who now makes his living as Otis' chaperone.

00:14:01.589 --> 00:14:06.200
As the adult Otis reflects from rehab, the
setting in which LaBeouf also wrote the screenplay,

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:10.490
he can't completely write his father off,
much as he may want to, and still finds himself

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:12.320
yearning for some sense of connection.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:17.300
Both critics and audiences heaped praise upon
Honey Boy, hailing LaBeouf's and co-star Noah

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:21.280
Jupe's performances and marveling at the depth
and honesty of LaBeouf's writing.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.780
Fortunately for the majority of people who
missed Honey Boy in theaters, the film will

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:28.000
begin streaming on Amazon Prime in February
of 2020.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:33.560
Adam Driver had an impressive year in 2019,
dominating the holiday box office with The

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:38.759
Rise of Skywalker and co-starring in Marriage
Story, which earned six Golden Globe nominations,

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:40.360
including a Best Actor nod for Driver.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:44.920
However, that same year, Driver also starred
in a couple other films that flew under most

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:49.550
people's radars, including the well-reviewed
political drama The Report, which opened in

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:52.000
limited release in November of 2019.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:56.760
The Report follows real-life U.S. Senate Investigator
Daniel Jones, who led an investigation into

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:02.200
the CIA's use of torture following the terrorist
attacks of September 11, 2001.

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:07.040
Co-starring alongside Driver in The Report
are Annette Bening as Senator Dianne Feinstein

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:11.080
and Jon Hamm as White House Chief
of Staff Denis McDonough, along with a host

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:12.620
of other recognizable faces.

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:17.080
Most reviews singled out Driver's performance
as exceptional, while many praised the film

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:20.300
for its straightforward and relentless depiction
of true events.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:21.560
Reviewer James Kendrick wrote:

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:26.900
"The Report is a particularly timely reminder
about the importance of the system of inter-governmental

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:31.470
checks and balances within a constitutional
democracy during a time when that system seems

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:32.900
more endangered than ever."

00:15:33.389 --> 00:15:37.180
The Report wasn't in theaters for long, but
fortunately for anyone who missed it, it's

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:38.980
now streaming on Amazon Prime.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:44.980
In 2021, Robert Pattinson will make his hopefully
triumphant return to major franchise fare

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:47.200
when he'll star as Bruce Wayne in The Batman.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.820
Until then, he's keeping plenty busy with
smaller, artistically ambitious films that

00:15:51.829 --> 00:15:56.240
challenge both actors and audiences alike,
such as the black and white psychological

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:57.680
horror film The Lighthouse.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:02.190
Starring opposite Willem Dafoe, The Lighthouse
follows a pair of lighthouse keepers who gradually

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:05.900
lose their grip on sanity after a storm traps
them on their remote island.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.300
"What."

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:14.820
"What."

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.220
Directed by The Witch filmmaker Robert Eggers,
who also co-wrote the screenplay with his

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:23.889
brother Max, The Lighthouse is a surreal and
disturbing "descent into madness," with standout

00:16:23.889 --> 00:16:26.060
performances from both Pattinson and Dafoe.

00:16:26.749 --> 00:16:31.480
It may be a little too bizarre for the casual
film fan one critic called it a, quote, "visceral

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:35.600
assault on the senses," with others warning
that it is, quote, "rough going" and "the

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:39.570
opposite of a crowd-pleaser" but these appear
to be features, not bugs, playing into the

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.420
overall effectiveness of the film.

